The two outside digits of a palindrome are mirrored, so the useful part is a pair on the left and its reversed pair on the right. I store pair counts for every prefix and suffix, then use each position as the center.

`left[i][a][b]` counts `a,b` before position `i`. The matching right count is `right[i][b][a]`. A string shorter than five is handled naturally, since no center has enough positions around it.

```python
class Solution:
    def countPalSubseq(self, s: str) -> int:
        mod = 1_000_000_007
        n = len(s)

        left = [[[0] * 10 for _ in range(10)] for _ in range(n + 1)]
        seen = [0] * 10

        for i, ch in enumerate(s):
            digit = ord(ch) - ord('0')
            for first in range(10):
                left[i + 1][first] = left[i][first][:]
                left[i + 1][first][digit] = (
                    left[i + 1][first][digit] + seen[first]
                ) % mod
            seen[digit] += 1

        right = [[[0] * 10 for _ in range(10)] for _ in range(n + 1)]
        seen = [0] * 10

        for i in range(n - 1, -1, -1):
            digit = ord(s[i]) - ord('0')
            for first in range(10):
                right[i][first] = right[i + 1][first][:]
                right[i][digit][first] = (
                    right[i][digit][first] + seen[first]
                ) % mod
            seen[digit] += 1

        answer = 0
        for center in range(2, n - 2):
            for first in range(10):
                for second in range(10):
                    answer = (
                        answer
                        + left[center][first][second]
                        * right[center + 1][second][first]
                    ) % mod

        return answer
```

Dry run on `"10301"`: using `3` as the center, the left side has pair `10` once and the right side has reversed pair `01` once, so this center contributes one palindrome.

The tables use extra memory, but the indexing is easy to check. Time complexity is `O(100n)`. Space complexity is `O(100n)`. The input is only read.

This keeps only counts for the part before the current character and the part after it. First, all two-digit subsequences on the right are built. During the main scan, the current character is removed from the right side before it is used as the center.

For a left pair `a,b`, the right pair must be `b,a`. The multiplication combines the choices on both sides without making the subsequence contiguous.

```csharp
public class Solution {
    public int CountPalSubseq(string s) {
        const long Mod = 1_000_000_007L;
        long[] rightOne = new long[10];
        long[,] rightPair = new long[10, 10];

        for (int i = s.Length - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
            int digit = s[i] - '0';
            for (int other = 0; other < 10; other++) {
                rightPair[digit, other] =
                    (rightPair[digit, other] + rightOne[other]) % Mod;
            }
            rightOne[digit]++;
        }

        long[] leftOne = new long[10];
        long[,] leftPair = new long[10, 10];
        long answer = 0;

        for (int i = 0; i < s.Length; i++) {
            int digit = s[i] - '0';

            rightOne[digit]--;
            for (int other = 0; other < 10; other++) {
                rightPair[digit, other] =
                    (rightPair[digit, other] - rightOne[other] + Mod) % Mod;
            }

            for (int first = 0; first < 10; first++) {
                for (int second = 0; second < 10; second++) {
                    answer = (answer +
                        leftPair[first, second] * rightPair[second, first]) % Mod;
                }
            }

            for (int first = 0; first < 10; first++) {
                leftPair[first, digit] =
                    (leftPair[first, digit] + leftOne[first]) % Mod;
            }
            leftOne[digit]++;
        }

        return (int) answer;
    }
}
```

The removal step matters. Without it, the center character could accidentally be counted as one of the two characters on its right. Inputs with fewer than five characters simply leave every product at zero.

Time complexity: `O(100n)`.
Space complexity: `O(100)`. The string itself is not modified.

There are only 100 choices for the first two digits. Once they are picked as `a` and `b`, the required shape is `a b ? b a`, where the middle character may be anything.

For each pair, `dp[k]` counts the ways to match the first `k` parts of this pattern. Updating from right to left keeps one input character from being selected twice.

```java
class Solution {
    public int countPalSubseq(String s) {
        final long MOD = 1_000_000_007L;
        long answer = 0;

        for (char first = '0'; first <= '9'; first++) {
            for (char second = '0'; second <= '9'; second++) {
                char[] pattern = {first, second, '?', second, first};
                long[] dp = new long[6];
                dp[0] = 1;

                for (int i = 0; i < s.length(); i++) {
                    char current = s.charAt(i);
                    for (int matched = 4; matched >= 0; matched--) {
                        if (matched == 2 || current == pattern[matched]) {
                            dp[matched + 1] = (dp[matched + 1] + dp[matched]) % MOD;
                        }
                    }
                }

                answer = (answer + dp[5]) % MOD;
            }
        }

        return (int) answer;
    }
}
```

A short string such as `"1234"` cannot produce a result because five positions are needed. The same code also handles repeated digits, including patterns such as `00000`.

Time: `O(100 * 5 * n)`, which is linear because the alphabet and pattern length are fixed.
Space: `O(1)` apart from the input string.

A length 5 palindrome always looks like `a b c b a`. While scanning the string, the tables store how many `a`, `a b`, `a b c`, and `a b c b` subsequences have been seen. When the current digit matches `a`, every matching four-character state creates a palindrome.

The updates are done in reverse order of the pattern length, so the current character is not used more than once.

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    int countPalSubseq(const string& s) {
        const long long MOD = 1'000'000'007;
        long long one[10] = {};
        long long two[10][10] = {};
        long long three[10][10] = {};
        long long four[10][10] = {};
        long long answer = 0;

        for (char ch : s) {
            int digit = ch - '0';

            for (int first = 0; first < 10; ++first) {
                for (int second = 0; second < 10; ++second) {
                    if (digit == first) {
                        answer = (answer + four[first][second]) % MOD;
                    }
                }
            }

            for (int first = 0; first < 10; ++first) {
                for (int second = 0; second < 10; ++second) {
                    if (digit == second) {
                        four[first][second] = (four[first][second] + three[first][second]) % MOD;
                    }
                    three[first][second] = (three[first][second] + two[first][second]) % MOD;
                    two[first][digit] = (two[first][digit] + one[first]) % MOD;
                }
            }

            one[digit] = (one[digit] + 1) % MOD;
        }

        return static_cast<int>(answer);
    }
};
```

For a quick dry run, after reading `1` then `0`, `two[1][0]` becomes 1. A later middle digit adds that count to the three-character state, and matching `0` and `1` digits finish the palindrome.

Time complexity: `O(100n)`.
Space complexity: `O(100)`. The input string is not changed.
